WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.638 --> 00:00:03.221
(upbeat music)

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.900
- Good morning, I'm Dr. Kyle Pruett

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.180
of the Yale Child Study Center,

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.240
also a member of the Early
Childhood Peace Consortium.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.290
And we are inviting you
to the second discussion

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:30.287
in the ECPC Raising
Children of Peace series.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.020
This is a natural topic for us to take on,

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.640
we are in our 10th year.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.410
We have been growing and
developing as an organization,

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:45.300
and it has become very
clear to us that the need

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:49.860
for the the youthful voice is essential

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.180
to our being able to spread
the very important message

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.600
about the importance of
peace in early childhood.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.600
The generation represented
on this panel has taught us

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:06.180
a great deal about this concept,

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.320
nothing about me without me.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.230
This is an organization of young people

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.170
and a generation of young
people who have given

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.990
a great deal of thought to how their voice

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:23.990
needs to be respected
because of the things

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.640
that they have to say about
the way that the world

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.640
is affecting them very directly.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.640
And this discussion is
about this intergenerational

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.520
conversation which is
essential to enlarging

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.460
our understanding about
the importance of peace

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.440
in the early years.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.420
The group that you see in the
gallery will be introduced

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:53.040
by Kyle King, who is a
rising senior at Yale.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.000
His invitation to his
colleagues has been essential

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.730
to making this conversation even possible.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:01.110
You will hear from each of them.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.050
He will introduce each of them in turn,

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.280
we will then be joined by Aditi Shrikhande

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.920
of UNICEF at the
end for a conversation

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.870
and round table roundup of ideas.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.930
And then we will finish
this morning's conversation

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.240
with thoughts about the future.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.560
Kyle King, thank you, the floor is yours.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:26.393
- Thanks, Kyle.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:29.910
Hello everybody, my name is Kyle King.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.820
As Kyle Pruett said, I'm
a rising senior at Yale,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.940
and I myself have been
doing advocacy work for

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.000
about eight years now, primarily
with two organizations,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.513
the International OCD
Foundation and UNICEF.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.870
And before I go ahead and
introduce everyone else

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.580
on this panel, I wanted
to take a minute to share

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.040
my own experiences with advocacy,

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.950
and hopefully say something
interesting as I talk about

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.620
my work with those two organizations.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.960
I started with the
International OCD Foundation

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.910
or the IOCDF when I was 13,

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:03.000
right after I finished
treatment for my own OCD.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.740
I was diagnosed with that when I was 12,

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.650
and after a pretty
grueling year of treatment,

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.290
I thought if I could do
anything to help someone else

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.750
with that disorder, I would do that.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.070
So I reached out to the IOCDF,

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:17.430
I asked if I could be an
advocate and they said sure.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.320
But then they gave me
no further instruction

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.760
on how to do that.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.280
My journey with UNICEF
started two years after

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:24.510
when I was in 9th grade.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.150
I was walking up a staircase
at the back of my high school

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.870
and I saw a poster of a boy
who is standing in the rubble

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:32.850
of what was his school.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:37.110
And here I was standing in
my well-funded public school

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:37.943
in South Florida,

00:03:37.943 --> 00:03:40.440
and there he was standing
in the remains of his.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.320
And I just was struck
with this thought that,

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.150
really there's no difference
between him and I,

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:47.400
I just happened to be born here

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.410
and he happened to be born there.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.240
And it felt so unfair to me

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.700
that because of some
arbitrary cosmic coin flip

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.180
I had access to all of these
resources and he didn't.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.800
So I felt like I needed
to do something about that

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.830
and I went and joined
my UNICEF club that day.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.620
And I've been doing work
with both those organizations

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.233
for like eight years, like I said.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:11.640
And I've gotten to see
firsthand how they interact

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.910
with youth advocates and they've
done it really differently.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.880
And I think there's something
to be said and learned from

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.270
the differences that these
two organizations show.

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:24.123
So starting with the IOCDF
since I started there first,

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.870
when I reached out when I was
13 as I kind of alluded to,

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:29.460
they provided me very little support

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:30.570
and very little guidance.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.670
And they didn't really tell
me what I could or couldn't do

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:33.503
as an advocate.

00:04:33.503 --> 00:04:36.420
They just said sure,
you can be an advocate.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.280
And I don't know if you
remember when you were 13,

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.500
but I wasn't a terribly
confident 13 year old,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.900
I didn't want to talk to adults very much.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.620
So for the first couple years
I really didn't do too much

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:49.410
with the IOCDF because
I didn't really know how

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.150
to use my voice.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.030
My experience with UNICEF
was really different.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.430
When I was 15 and wanted to join

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:57.840
and start working with UNICEF,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.140
I just went and joined the
club in my high school.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:02.940
And that was a really
easy way to get started.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.010
And had there not been a
club at my high school,

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:08.730
UNICEF has full webpages
dedicated to helping kids

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.980
start a club in the United States.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:12.810
And even beyond the clubs program,

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.090
UNICEF USA has positions baked into

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:17.733
their corporate structure
for youth advocates.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.910
So that's kinda the long
way of saying that UNICEF

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.980
does what the IOCDF didn't,

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.860
which made it really easy for me to begin

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.540
my advocacy journey with them.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.040
And as a result, for
the first couple years

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.283
of my advocacy work, I
mainly worked with UNICEF.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:34.890
And as I kind of worked my way through

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:36.960
the different advocacy
positions at UNICEF,

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.580
I began to build up my voice

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.520
and I began to become a lot
more confident as an advocate.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.370
And consequently, I
began to have more ideas

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:46.890
and really want to implement
them more strongly.

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:49.770
And that's when I began to
realize that the structure

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:52.530
that UNICEF had laid out for
me and that the IOCDF hadn't

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:54.660
was a bit of a double-edged sword.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.540
Because over the years there's
been a lot of times at UNICEF

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:00.970
where the structure began
to feel a bit confining,

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:02.580
and I began to feel boxed in

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.190
and I felt like I couldn't
really use my voice

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:06.903
as authentically as I wanted to.

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:10.440
So looking back at the
IOCDF that experience

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.030
was totally different.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:15.810
Because after I had kind of
found my voice at UNICEF,

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.660
the lack of structure and
the laws of fair governance

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:22.140
over there began to
feel like a huge asset.

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.720
I could do the projects
that I wanted to do

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.430
as authentically as I wanted to do them

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:28.980
and I would try certain things
and sometimes they would fail

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.650
and sometimes they would
be great successes.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:34.440
But it was really
incentivizing that I was able

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.133
to do the advocacy work that
I really truly wanted to do.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.520
And I don't mean to disparage
either organization,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.280
they both meant a lot to
me as I've been growing up.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.040
And they both taught me a lot
of really important things.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.530
But one thing that I wanted to communicate

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.810
to all of you as you look to engage youth

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.720
in whatever organization
you're representing here,

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:57.540
it's necessary to meet
youth where they're at.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.720
Because before a young
person finds their voice

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.970
and when they are getting
started in advocacy,

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.520
it's really daunting, it's
a really scary experience.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.890
And it's really important to
have those easy to get into

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:11.820
positions like UNICEF did
to help them along their way

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.800
and help them find that voice.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.050
But once they find that voice,

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:19.110
it's really important to
genuinely and earnestly

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.150
hear that voice out and
allow a young person

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.543
to express their voice less
they might lose it entirely.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:28.203
Okay, that's a lot from me.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:30.840
The two Kyles have spoken too much.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:32.610
It's time to pass it on to
the other youth advocates

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:33.443
on the call.

00:07:33.443 --> 00:07:35.970
Just by way of context, we all met,

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:39.870
me and the other three young
youth advocates at an event

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:43.440
that was hosted by UNICEF
last September in New York.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.510
We didn't know each other
prior to getting to New York

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.790
and then we met in New York and we spent

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:48.870
three days together.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:52.050
And in those three days we
grew so close that I could,

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:53.040
oh, I don't know,

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:55.860
ask them to participate
on a webinar on a whim,

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:57.720
which I think is pretty
cool that we became

00:07:57.720 --> 00:07:59.580
such good friends so quickly.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.070
And I think it's because we
all have a lot in common.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:03.240
So without further ado,

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.880
the first youth advocate
who would be speaking today

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:06.810
is Penelope Lea.

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.850
Penelope is a climate justice advocate

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:10.350
primarily from Norway.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.450
And she's one of the most
accomplished advocates

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.940
I've ever had the pleasure
of meeting in my life.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:18.450
And she's also one of the
sweetest people in the world

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:21.150
and I think you'll learn a lot from her.

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.523
So Penelope, your time to shine.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:27.900
- Thank you so much Kyle
and thank you for your talk.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.910
Thank you for having me.

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:32.610
I started my activism when I was eight.

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:36.960
I started because I was and
I still am sick and tired

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.120
of violence and inequalities.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.560
And the fight for climate justice,

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:43.170
the fight for climate
action is the fight against

00:08:43.170 --> 00:08:44.403
these very forces.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.130
Before I started my activism,

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:50.220
I experienced violence in close relations.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:52.500
And this experience
actually became my entry

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:54.150
into climate activism.

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:58.620
It fueled my need to speak
up and it strengthened

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.380
my impatience in the fight for a peaceful

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.850
and safe future for
everything I love, the people,

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.013
the nature and life itself.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:11.973
And this impatience of mine
has only grown since then.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:15.570
As we know, climate and nature changes

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.540
are an amplifier of all
other crisis we face

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.550
as humans beings.

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:22.740
The climate and nature
crisis is inevitably linked

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:26.970
to the world's security situation,
to economics and poverty.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:30.180
It's tightly linked to how
we understand ourselves

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:34.230
and our own history, and
of course to every single

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:36.810
one of the human and children's rights.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.723
I believe to understand
it another way is naive.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.260
Therefore, as a climate justice activist,

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:46.110
I've found myself working
with several of these issues

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:47.820
over the past 10 years.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:50.760
And in this talk I wanna
share some of my experiences

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:52.050
with you.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.440
But first, I would love
to mention some background

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.720
on the conversation
around climate and nature

00:09:57.720 --> 00:09:59.310
in my home country,

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:01.890
to give you in a better idea of how it is

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.390
to be a climate activist here.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:08.070
I believe I can safely say
that the debate around climate

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:11.160
and nature changes is the
most polarized and heated

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:13.110
that there is in Norway.

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:14.640
Norway is a small country

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.980
with five and a half million people.

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:20.760
In 1969, we found oil and gas.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.160
And since then we have
built our whole economy

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.010
on oil and gas production and export.

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:27.720
Just before the summer holiday,

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:31.203
19 new oil licenses were
approved by our government.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:34.200
And there is currently no political party

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.510
that stands a chance
of forming a government

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.450
that wants to stop giving
out new oil licenses

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:43.563
and move away from fossil
fuel dependency fast enough.

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:48.780
In 33 years, our government has only cut

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:51.053
our greenhouse gas emissions by 4.7%.

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:54.660
We are one of the countries in Europe

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.670
with the most climate deniers.

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.040
One in four people in Norway don't believe

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:01.390
that climate change is
caused by human actions.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:06.570
There is no excuse for
Norway to delay change.

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:09.930
We have the solutions, the
resources, the possibilities,

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:12.963
the peace, and the people
in power have had the time.

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:17.700
We keep on living with
and enjoying the freedoms

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.350
people before us have fought for,

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:22.650
democracies, weekends,
eight hour work days,

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.300
they did it for us.

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.640
And still we seem to think
that it is impossible

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:28.860
to change the systems we live in.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:31.200
To me, that is double standard.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.473
We can change what we view
as political impossible.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:38.610
But to be able to create a
better world and to make people

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:41.400
aware that it is in
fact possible to change,

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:44.250
it's been proven that we need
the voices of young people

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:46.650
and we need to ease the
struggles young people face

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:50.220
in their advocacy for basic human rights.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.440
So now I'm gonna share
some struggles with you,

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.843
me and my friends face in our work.

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:59.220
We often talk about how
young people need to speak

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:02.250
truth to power, but we rarely
talk about what happens

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:05.523
when power speaks to you,
the hate you might receive.

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:08.760
Lots of activists live in
places where it's not safe

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:11.490
to speak your mind or
be politically engaged.

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:14.160
And even the ones of
us that live in safety

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:16.710
can be unsafe at some point.

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:18.690
We need to hear the
voices of these activists,

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:20.340
we need to hear the voices of activists

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:22.290
from the frontline communities.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:25.050
We desperately need that representation,

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:27.540
but we have to make sure
that these spaces are safe

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.730
for young people to be represented in,

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:34.230
not just at the stage but
afterwards, you need to follow up

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:36.450
and make sure to the
best of your abilities

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.100
that they have the support
and safety that they need.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:43.710
One of the ways to do
this I believe is to build

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:48.270
more political organizations
for young children, community.

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:50.880
When I was younger it felt life important

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:53.730
to be able to use my voice and get heard.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:56.430
For me it was a question
of all or nothing.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:58.170
I needed to use my voice and feel like

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:01.350
I had the say in my own
life and my own future.

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:02.673
I needed to contribute.

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:05.490
But when you're young
it's often very difficult

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.280
to find organizations
where you can do just that.

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:10.710
Where you're not just
questioned because of your age

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:15.360
but brought up and valued
where everyone understands

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.940
that your thoughts are
your own and filled with

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.073
as much integrity as if you were older.

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:24.000
I started my activism in
an organization like this,

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.950
but we need help building more of them.

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:32.010
Youth organization and frontline
communities needs funding.

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:34.020
The money is not going where it's needed.

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:36.510
It's in the hands of huge
companies and businesses

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:40.080
serving exploitive systems,
gaining the very few,

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:41.880
but not with young change makers

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:44.730
or the frontline communities
fighting for a better world.

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:47.370
And we need to change the conversation

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:49.260
around funding as well.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:52.410
Too often my friends and I
are met with huge skepticism

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:54.210
when we advocate for climate finance

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:57.330
or funding of youth advocacy communities.

00:13:57.330 --> 00:14:00.120
But one needs some level
of financial security

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:02.253
to be able to work for other freedoms.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:06.150
At the UN General Assembly,

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:08.190
Priyanka, Dominique, Kyle and I,

00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:10.350
repeatedly heard the statement,

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:13.173
if you don't have a chair at
the table, bring your own.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:15.930
That is not possible.

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:19.127
If you're not invited and
you don't have the resources,

00:14:19.127 --> 00:14:22.170
it's of course not possible
to grab your own chair

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:23.003
at the table.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.190
And it's not just about
having a chair at the table,

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:28.020
but having a role at the table,

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.940
getting opportunities to speak, talk,

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:32.913
follow negotiations
actively, ask questions.

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.110
We need to talk about the conversation,

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:39.960
the connection between human
rights and children's rights,

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:42.870
peace, security, and climate change.

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:44.820
And a lot of young people
in the climate movement

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:46.710
want to talk about these connections

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:48.300
because it's crucial to understand

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:50.910
to be able to implement the just solutions

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:53.493
and have the just transition we need.

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:59.100
But it is of course viewpoints seldom,

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:00.870
it's hard to get through in media.

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:03.600
So we need help from every one of you

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:06.480
to bring this conversation
forward so we can move forward

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:08.130
in the fight for climate justice.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:13.560
I believe activism is
in its greatest form,

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:16.023
peace building in its nature.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:20.490
We might never know which of
our actions may lead to change

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:23.490
or may make more people
follow in our footsteps.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:27.213
Therefore, everything we do
may turn out to be important.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:30.390
When I hear myself speak now,

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:33.270
I realize that a lot of points I have

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:36.660
have to do with building
safety for young people

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:37.953
in one way or another.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:41.880
I believe to be able to
build peace in any form

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:45.810
you need safety, peace
is in my experience,

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:47.340
not always peaceful,

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:50.070
but you need safety to
be able to fight for it.

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:50.903
Thank you.

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:55.320
- Thank you Penelope, that was incredible.

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:59.280
And when you mentioned that phrase like,

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:02.613
if there's no seat at the
table or bring your own chair,

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:06.570
I remember hearing that
phrase so often that it became

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:11.570
somewhat hollow because like
you said, in my experience,

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:14.400
it's not always as simple
as just bringing a chair.

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:16.560
And a lot of times there
are people at the table

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.360
who don't want to hear
from a young person,

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:21.150
even if you get there in the first place.

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:24.540
Our next speaker is Dominique Ogreanu,

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:26.910
which I hope I pronounce correctly.

00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:30.900
And she's a young advocate from Romania.

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:33.030
When Kyle asked me what advocates

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:35.070
I'd wanna have on this panel,

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:36.690
I immediately thought of Dominique

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:40.770
because I've met a lot of
advocates through the years

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:44.190
and I don't know if I've met
anyone else whose emotion

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:47.520
and raw passion for the
subjects that they advocate on

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:50.373
is as evident as it is
when Dominique speaks.

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:52.845
They are really special

00:16:52.845 --> 00:16:56.013
and I'm really excited for this talk.

00:16:58.530 --> 00:17:00.570
- Hi, thank you so much Kyle.

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:03.510
Oh, that was such a nice
introduction I wasn't expecting,

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:07.680
definitely no pressure on
me to deliver that emotion.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:09.900
So I'm very grateful to
be here with all of you.

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:11.160
I'm so grateful for Penelope

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:12.647
for sharing her experience before me

00:17:12.647 --> 00:17:15.810
'cause a lot of the things
that she has touched upon also

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:19.470
they'll find themselves
throughout my talk as well.

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:21.150
So when I was first invited here

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:23.970
the first question that
popped into my head was,

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:25.260
what is peace?

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:27.330
Because we hear this word everywhere

00:17:27.330 --> 00:17:29.390
and it's on TV all the time,

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:32.250
we hear it at high level
dialogues at school.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:34.680
So in preparation for the
talk and for my speech,

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:36.750
I ask the children that I work with,

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:40.320
how do they define the concept,
what is peace for them?

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:42.840
And I observed that as
their age got higher,

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:45.450
their responses were
more complicated, right?

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:50.450
They were sprinkling this
according to this academic paper

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:52.953
or it's actually hard to define.

00:17:53.941 --> 00:17:56.190
And less of their answers
came from their souls

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:57.870
and too many from the external forces

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.660
that they have interacted with.

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:03.270
So one very interesting
definition that I heard

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:06.900
and it was from from one of
my colleagues that is 10,

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:11.900
was that peace is the
possibility to play without fear.

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:16.800
And it was very important
because many children

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:18.900
trade their childhoods to fight

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:22.290
because the problems are so pressing.

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:25.920
So fear, fear is one of
the worst motivators,

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:29.280
but it is one that brings
children to advocacy,

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:33.360
be it fear of not being accepted
to fear of being killed.

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:34.920
And upon hearing the word fear,

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:37.500
I immediately thought about resilience.

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:39.330
How many times did we as youth advocates

00:18:39.330 --> 00:18:41.190
hear that we are resilient?

00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:44.370
How many times did you
as an adult hear it?

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:47.490
And resilience has become
code word for silence.

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:49.920
You are so resilient because
you can take injustice

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:53.550
so easily, you absorb it
and you don't make a sound.

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:57.150
You don't express the the grief
you feel, the worthlessness,

00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:59.970
the exhaustion because you can take it.

00:18:59.970 --> 00:19:02.310
And this upholds the
idea of individuality.

00:19:02.310 --> 00:19:05.760
Like you are the
responsible for your mood.

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:07.710
And it is through this
that we allow each other

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:11.280
to rid of the collective duty
of protecting one another.

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:13.500
It is this that becomes an obstacle

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:16.890
to intergenerational
communication to peace.

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:20.220
Because peace means
taking care of each other.

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:22.950
Now, to understand how youth advocates,

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:24.240
how to help youth advocates,

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:25.830
you must understand their journey.

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:28.170
And while what I'm about to to talk about

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:30.240
might not be a universal account

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:33.630
since everybody has a unique way,

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:36.900
it is based on my experience
and on those of the people

00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:38.640
that I truly love.

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:40.350
I truly believe that
there are three feelings

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:43.170
that you encounter at all
times in your activist path.

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:47.010
One is rage, the other is
loneliness coupled with belonging

00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:50.250
which I name as the
feeling of in betweenness,

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:51.750
and then there's hope.

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:53.730
These coexist at any
time during your path,

00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:56.640
they don't alchemize
from one into the other,

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:00.120
they uplift each other, they
spring from one another.

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:01.413
So let's start with rage.

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:06.150
I often felt that my
country was at war with me.

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:09.030
That it's hatefulness
for dissenting bodies

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:11.760
pierce through my soul and
it extracted all the light

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:14.520
and the love I was
desperately trying to harbor.

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:16.710
So determined to banish me from contesting

00:20:16.710 --> 00:20:18.960
their hegemonic practices.

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:21.660
Different systems like
nationalism, capitalism,

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:24.510
heteronormativity, invaded
the minds of others,

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:27.270
my teachers, my colleagues, my friends,

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:29.820
isolating me from those
I was fighting for.

00:20:29.820 --> 00:20:31.980
And it is not easy to care for a community

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:34.800
that seeks to destroy your will and agency

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:37.320
and to convince you of your worthlessness

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:39.750
because these oppressive systems
seeping through the minds

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:44.250
of those we love show up as
Penelope said, as immutable.

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:47.370
They are power impossible to
contest without a movement.

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:50.250
So with this in mind in the
universe of museum arrangements,

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:53.820
architects sat down, created
a city at the intersection

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:56.550
of the public and the private
and asked the vital question,

00:20:56.550 --> 00:21:00.150
what if we put together people
broken by the same systems

00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:02.040
and just let them love each other?

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:04.620
So in a room with a circular
table on the third floor

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:06.690
of a building in the city of Foley,

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:09.480
a group of students from
different parts of the world

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:11.790
and their professor met
every day to discuss theory

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:13.530
as a way of survival.

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:15.870
All having been hated by
their countries at a point

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:19.020
in their lives or aching for
connection and understanding,

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:21.210
they explored the musings
of those in history

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:24.420
who share their struggles,
unintentionally creating good

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:26.850
transgenerational knowledge hub.

00:21:26.850 --> 00:21:28.290
This is how you build a movement.

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:30.750
You bring the outcast, the
thinkers, the believers,

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:33.030
the reformers, the revolutionaries,

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:36.120
those who want to rebuild the
systems that are hurting them

00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:38.490
and you make them a safe haven.

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:41.430
And in this place of
safety you let them revolt,

00:21:41.430 --> 00:21:44.160
you conspire with them, you
make plans, you organize,

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:46.203
you let them discover their identity.

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:51.330
I remember the immense
rightful rage that I felt

00:21:51.330 --> 00:21:53.610
when in my freshman year of
high school my colleagues,

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:56.520
my LGBTQ colleagues came
to me and told me that

00:21:56.520 --> 00:21:58.740
we can't keep each other from dying

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:01.080
as they were building
their own networks of care

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:06.080
in absence of support from our teachers.

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:10.860
Because what else can you feel
when your planet is on fire,

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:13.200
or when you get denied
lifesaving procedures,

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.180
or when you're outed and marginalized,

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:18.240
what are you supposed to feel if not rage?

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:22.350
We encounter unfairness at
different times in our life.

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:24.570
But the first feeling is that
we are alone in this hurt.

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:27.450
That we are the first to experience this.

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:30.480
This is where intergenerational
support comes in.

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:32.280
Visibility is everything.

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:34.500
As you discover people who think like you

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:38.220
and who for decades have been
involved in the same activism.

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:40.290
Activism is a love story.

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:43.800
Beyond the anger, there
is a group, a movement,

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:46.920
a collective to which you
belong and with which you fight.

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:49.327
And in the darkest of
nights when you're defeated,

00:22:49.327 --> 00:22:51.720
it is the people that become your fuel,

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:54.600
the solidarity and support
you are overcome with.

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:57.450
When you are in tears and
at the brink of giving up,

00:22:57.450 --> 00:23:00.300
you look up to them and they
reassure you that they fought

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:03.330
through worse times and
that you have the tools

00:23:03.330 --> 00:23:06.840
and the foundation to fight
whatever your enemy is.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:10.590
But above all, you have the
promise of no matter what,

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:11.880
no matter what happens,

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:14.580
intergenerational support has your back.

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:18.600
In these spaces of safety you
learn to choose joy over doom.

00:23:18.600 --> 00:23:19.860
You are free to play,

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:22.260
you construct the safe haven of peace,

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:26.040
this manmade circle of unity.

00:23:26.040 --> 00:23:28.390
And then you take it
outside to the real world.

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:32.670
Soon enough you become
a safe space for others.

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:36.030
The peace inside of
yourself reflects outwards.

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:38.850
While you constantly wonder
what really is the impact

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:41.250
you're making, your
caretaker will sit you down

00:23:41.250 --> 00:23:42.810
and tell you, I don't
understand this issue

00:23:42.810 --> 00:23:45.180
you're advocating for yet
but I'm willing to learn.

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:47.580
Your friend will ask you to
go to a protest together.

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:49.440
Your teacher will sign your petition.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:52.200
The youth you encounter will
ask you how to get involved.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:56.163
You'll become a catalyzer,
a unifier, an antidote.

00:23:57.210 --> 00:24:01.380
All we really want as youth is
to be safe, to be understood,

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:05.370
to see our loved ones and
caretakers enjoy safety as well.

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:08.550
Now for this to happen, adults
need to decenter themselves

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:10.380
to come into our communities,

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:12.180
to bring the power table that we've been

00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:14.790
talking about before into our communities

00:24:14.790 --> 00:24:18.600
and let us work together
instead of against each other.

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:22.020
While safety continues to be
seen in securitization terms

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:25.200
like countries, borders, national spirit,

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:28.200
we must remember that these
are abstract concepts,

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:32.490
but people are real, feelings are real.

00:24:32.490 --> 00:24:34.170
When we try to define peace,

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:38.280
perhaps we should ask
ourselves how peace feels

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:40.590
rather than what it means.

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:43.380
So I want to leave you with
a question before I end,

00:24:43.380 --> 00:24:46.140
how does peace feel to you?

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:50.310
And I'll start, for me
peace feels like relief.

00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:53.880
It feels like taking a breath
after holding it for so long

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.550
because finally in the
dark room that you thought

00:24:56.550 --> 00:25:00.510
you were in and that my
country was desperately

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:03.360
trying to put me in at the
beginning of the story,

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:07.680
there suddenly are people's
hands to hold, light garlands

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:09.003
and a lot of hope.

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:12.432
That was it, back to you, Kyle.

00:25:12.432 --> 00:25:16.560
- Yeah, Dominique, you more than lived up

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:18.243
to the introduction I gave you.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:23.460
I'm reflecting on the question,

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:24.293
what does peace feel like?

00:25:24.293 --> 00:25:26.430
That's a complicated question to answer.

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:29.370
I think I would say like ease in a way

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:33.180
I am thinking of my own
experiences being marginalized

00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:36.720
for having a mental disorder
and walking into a place

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:39.633
and not being marginalized for
that feels like ease to me.

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:42.510
But I'd have to think about it further.

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:45.540
Our third youth advocate
is Priyanka Lalla.

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:49.020
She's an advocate from
Trinidad and Tobago.

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:50.910
And just a cool part of her biography

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:53.040
that I wanted to highlight
before she speaks is that

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:56.767
she is an author and an
illustrator of a book called

00:25:56.767 --> 00:26:00.330
"Svara's Ocean Adventure"
that she published in 2022.

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:04.110
And I feel like it's a little
unfair that someone has like

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:07.050
that much talent like they
can draw and they can write.

00:26:07.050 --> 00:26:09.300
I don't know, it just kind of made me mad.

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:11.673
But Priyanka, the floor is yours.

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:14.340
- Thank you so much.

00:26:14.340 --> 00:26:17.340
Good morning everyone, and
thank you for having me.

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:20.610
Kyle's story resonates with
me because when I was about

00:26:20.610 --> 00:26:24.480
six years old when I saw a
young boy about eight years old

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:26.940
selling vegetables with his parents,

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:30.180
I told my grandfather that
I wanted to be his friend.

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:32.283
I felt that if we knew each other,

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:36.120
he would live my same life.

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:40.360
And growing up in a small
island he has allowed me

00:26:41.220 --> 00:26:44.010
to have a close look
at the world around me.

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:46.950
It's given me an incredibly unique

00:26:46.950 --> 00:26:50.400
and invaluable appreciation
for nature and therefore

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:52.200
a passion to protect it.

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:56.130
The beauty of my island was
mine to explore and cherish.

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:59.100
And this is truly where my advocacy began.

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:03.480
In 2017, at the age of 10,
I came to the realization

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:05.910
that this narrative of
protecting and preserving

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:09.870
the environment was not
practiced by most of our region.

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:13.080
Evidence of dumping was
discovered in the mountains

00:27:13.080 --> 00:27:15.270
of debris and pollution washed up after

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:18.360
the devastating hurricanes Irma and Maria.

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:20.130
I felt compelled to take action.

00:27:20.130 --> 00:27:22.860
And I knew that if I wanted to see change,

00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:25.860
I could not go around
asking others to change.

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:29.880
I needed to start on my own
and I needed to lead by example

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:31.980
to mitigate the risks of my country

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:34.500
and region's peace and security.

00:27:34.500 --> 00:27:37.020
My advocacy began with education.

00:27:37.020 --> 00:27:40.110
I believe that our school
curriculums did not sufficiently

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:42.480
address climate and environmental issues.

00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:44.670
And certainly there was little focus

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:47.250
on meaningful sustainability.

00:27:47.250 --> 00:27:51.270
Our economy similar to Penelope,
is based on oil and gas.

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:55.200
And we have little
attempts at diversification

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:58.140
to ensure a regenerative economy.

00:27:58.140 --> 00:28:01.740
Young people need more
opportunities to thrive

00:28:01.740 --> 00:28:05.400
and this has led to brain
drain over the years.

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:08.550
This is a threat to our country's peace.

00:28:08.550 --> 00:28:12.240
I decided that I would be
the change I wanted to see

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:14.310
to build a more sustainable country

00:28:14.310 --> 00:28:17.010
and a more sustainable region.

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:19.980
And this inspired my
blog, Zero Waste Living.

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:22.260
I learned a great deal
from my grandparents

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:24.180
who lived sustainably because they learned

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:26.820
from their parents and that influenced

00:28:26.820 --> 00:28:29.550
the way in which my family already lived.

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:31.530
I guess we can say this is an example

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:33.660
of intergenerational education

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:35.760
that was happening in my home.

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:39.300
And this quest for sustainability
led to the creation

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:41.340
of the Zero Waste Lunchbox,

00:28:41.340 --> 00:28:43.800
an initiative that I started at my school.

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:45.900
I encouraged students to pack a lunch

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:48.330
with no disposables or plastic.

00:28:48.330 --> 00:28:51.090
And my dream was that
one day this would become

00:28:51.090 --> 00:28:53.280
a Caribbean wide initiative.

00:28:53.280 --> 00:28:56.550
I simultaneously created
a Zero Waste blog.

00:28:56.550 --> 00:28:59.160
I researched and wrote
about how we can make

00:28:59.160 --> 00:29:02.850
almost every aspect of our
lives more sustainable.

00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:05.670
And I realized that
meaningful climate action

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:08.400
was not difficult for my small island.

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:10.230
We did not need to reinvent the wheel

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:12.990
because the solutions already existed.

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:15.600
As just a few decades ago our grandparents

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:17.580
were living sustainably.

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:20.580
When I became a national
child rights ambassador,

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:23.370
I found a connection
between climate change

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:26.820
and every aspect of child
rights and human rights.

00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:29.100
But what stood out to me the most was that

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:32.430
along with child rights
came responsibilities.

00:29:32.430 --> 00:29:34.950
We have the right to a healthy and safe

00:29:34.950 --> 00:29:36.840
and thriving environment,

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:40.260
but it is our responsibility
to maintain and ensure that

00:29:40.260 --> 00:29:43.530
for ourselves and for future generations.

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:47.190
And after around four years
of consistent advocacy,

00:29:47.190 --> 00:29:49.770
young people started
to contact me asking me

00:29:49.770 --> 00:29:52.260
what gave me the courage to do this

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:54.870
or how they could get involved.

00:29:54.870 --> 00:29:58.320
And it became so clear to me
that there was an audience

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:00.030
of young people out there,

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:03.180
but they lack the opportunities
and the support they needed

00:30:03.180 --> 00:30:05.850
to realize their true potential.

00:30:05.850 --> 00:30:09.630
And one of my successes was
becoming a UNICEF youth advocate

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:12.960
where I was able to meet
young, enthusiastic people like

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:15.180
Kyle, Penelope and Dominique,

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:18.390
who truly are passionate
about their cause.

00:30:18.390 --> 00:30:21.000
And working with UNICEF and
its partners has helped me

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:23.190
to learn more about the climate crisis

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:26.970
and how every aspect of our
lives is affected by it.

00:30:26.970 --> 00:30:29.580
I would say that one of
my biggest accomplishments

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:32.310
is my participation in
the regional consultation

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:34.710
for Latin America and the Caribbean

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:39.180
on drafting the general
observation number 26,

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:42.120
on children's rights and the environment

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:44.883
with a special focus on climate change.

00:30:46.020 --> 00:30:47.400
And after this conference,

00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:51.150
my eight year old sisters Vara
and I wrote and illustrated

00:30:51.150 --> 00:30:54.630
and published as Svara's Ocean Adventures.

00:30:54.630 --> 00:30:57.870
Our objective was truly climate education,

00:30:57.870 --> 00:31:02.220
engaging, empowering, and
inspiring climate education,

00:31:02.220 --> 00:31:05.250
giving children the solutions
that they could implement

00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:07.620
even at such a young age.

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:10.350
But I still believe that
we have an uphill battle

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:12.810
in small island developing states.

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:16.260
We are still faced with the
issues of general disconnect

00:31:16.260 --> 00:31:19.590
between real climate action
and what is being taught

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:22.230
in schools and local programs.

00:31:22.230 --> 00:31:25.920
Climate and environmental issues
are taught in science class

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:28.920
without any reference to
how we are contributors

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:31.080
on a daily basis.

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:34.440
Many young people continue
to use single use plastics

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:37.440
and are often encouraged
to start beach cleanups.

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:40.500
However, they do not realize
that they are contributing

00:31:40.500 --> 00:31:41.910
to the problems.

00:31:41.910 --> 00:31:44.580
What is required is not cleaning up.

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:46.560
Every time but a reduction,

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:50.130
but in short term and
ultimately a ban of these items

00:31:50.130 --> 00:31:52.773
and environmental laws to be enforced.

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:56.460
It is our peril if this is ignored,

00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:59.490
our vulnerability is always
evident when we are hit

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:01.200
by natural disasters.

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:03.180
Entire communities are destroyed

00:32:03.180 --> 00:32:05.220
causing children to be taken out of school

00:32:05.220 --> 00:32:07.020
to support their families.

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:09.300
And when they are taken
out of the education system

00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:12.540
they are forced into child
labor, child marriages.

00:32:12.540 --> 00:32:13.890
And due to less income,

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:15.990
they are usually left with poor nutrition

00:32:15.990 --> 00:32:17.823
and no way out of this cycle.

00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:22.080
Our rising crime rate, food
insecurity, increasing figures

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:25.470
in non-communicable
diseases and urgent need

00:32:25.470 --> 00:32:28.470
for transformative education
are some of the factors

00:32:28.470 --> 00:32:31.350
that jeopardize regional peace.

00:32:31.350 --> 00:32:33.240
People fail to make these connections

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:36.000
between the reality and the climate crisis

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:40.110
which we feel and see the
implications of every day.

00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:43.020
We cannot have sustainable
development and peace

00:32:43.020 --> 00:32:45.420
under these circumstances.

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:48.180
And while I have seen a
growing number of young people

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:51.990
joining the conversation or
taking action in small ways,

00:32:51.990 --> 00:32:53.520
it is not enough.

00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:56.460
In a country with 1.4 million people,

00:32:56.460 --> 00:32:59.070
the numbers in the
conversation are very small,

00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:01.050
a few hundred maybe.

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:04.140
I also question the quality of involvement

00:33:04.140 --> 00:33:06.840
as sometimes young people
invited as what appears

00:33:06.840 --> 00:33:11.190
to be tokenism for photo ops
on preparation of reports

00:33:11.190 --> 00:33:14.250
for funding agencies
and plans for meaningful

00:33:14.250 --> 00:33:17.973
long-term engagement
seem to be theoretical.

00:33:19.020 --> 00:33:22.590
I truly believe in
intergenerational action

00:33:22.590 --> 00:33:25.530
where we must work
collectively and in harmony

00:33:25.530 --> 00:33:28.500
to create affordable,
accessible, and sustainable

00:33:28.500 --> 00:33:32.820
education systems with
capacity building opportunities

00:33:32.820 --> 00:33:36.480
and programs for young
people to truly be engaged

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:39.570
and to know that they have
a right to have meaningful

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:42.900
seat at the table but there's always hope.

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:43.733
Thank you.

00:33:45.540 --> 00:33:47.640
- That was also just an incredible speech.

00:33:48.690 --> 00:33:52.530
I was just reflecting again
on the seat at the table

00:33:52.530 --> 00:33:54.600
and the issue of tokenization
that you brought up

00:33:54.600 --> 00:33:57.720
'cause I felt that multiple
times through my advocacy.

00:33:57.720 --> 00:34:01.110
Just feeling like I was
invited to an event,

00:34:01.110 --> 00:34:03.060
someone took a picture of
me and that picture ended up

00:34:03.060 --> 00:34:05.970
on a PDF online somewhere.

00:34:05.970 --> 00:34:07.830
But I didn't really get to
say anything at the event

00:34:07.830 --> 00:34:09.903
other than just cheese.

00:34:12.030 --> 00:34:13.683
Kyle Pruett, the floor is yours.

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:16.110
- Thank you, Kyle.

00:34:18.660 --> 00:34:21.180
I'm writing down pearls as fast as I can.

00:34:21.180 --> 00:34:24.180
I feel very privileged to be sitting here

00:34:24.180 --> 00:34:28.170
with this remarkable
group of young people.

00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:31.143
I'm struck by their
passion, their impatience,

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:38.150
the problems at the table that
they so accurately highlight.

00:34:38.460 --> 00:34:42.780
This conversation is I hope
an antidote to that problem

00:34:42.780 --> 00:34:44.340
at the table.

00:34:44.340 --> 00:34:48.570
The hope that I believe
Dominique spoke about

00:34:48.570 --> 00:34:51.930
reminded me I believe Bill McKibben,

00:34:51.930 --> 00:34:55.413
the very active climate activist,

00:34:56.910 --> 00:35:01.440
talked about hope as a verb
with its sleeves rolled up.

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:06.240
And each of you have talked
about action, passion,

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:08.790
your competence is very obvious

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:12.000
and we are looking forward to hearing

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:15.540
not only more from you,
but we're now going to

00:35:15.540 --> 00:35:18.780
have the treat of listening
to Aditi Shrikhande,

00:35:18.780 --> 00:35:22.290
who is the early childhood
development program officer

00:35:22.290 --> 00:35:23.580
from UNICEF.

00:35:23.580 --> 00:35:27.030
And Aditi has the delightful
task of summarizing

00:35:27.030 --> 00:35:28.770
some of the things that she has learned.

00:35:28.770 --> 00:35:32.610
I hope she is as perturbed as
I am by the wonderful things

00:35:32.610 --> 00:35:35.613
that you have brought to
our table today, Aditi.

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:38.580
- Thank you, Kyle.

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:41.220
Yes, I do wanna echo exactly
what you've just said.

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:46.220
This has been so fascinating
and I really appreciated

00:35:46.320 --> 00:35:48.510
learning from each of the
experiences that you've shared.

00:35:48.510 --> 00:35:53.510
So big thank you to all
of our presenters today.

00:35:53.790 --> 00:35:57.030
And I think some of the
things that you spoke about

00:35:57.030 --> 00:35:59.460
really caught onto a lot of the key themes

00:35:59.460 --> 00:36:03.540
that at UNICEF as well
we try to incorporate

00:36:03.540 --> 00:36:07.830
into peace building things
like community leadership

00:36:07.830 --> 00:36:12.830
and community engagement,
mental health and wellbeing,

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:15.930
and empowering young people to be leaders

00:36:15.930 --> 00:36:18.780
and supporting their
organizations and initiatives.

00:36:18.780 --> 00:36:22.380
I think these were all things
that I heard from all of you.

00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:25.500
And at the heart of UNICEF's
peace building work,

00:36:25.500 --> 00:36:29.193
this is what we strive to do as well,

00:36:30.300 --> 00:36:34.650
to take a child centered
social services approach

00:36:34.650 --> 00:36:39.390
and to bridge community and
intergenerational divides

00:36:39.390 --> 00:36:42.330
to reduce social tension and address

00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:45.090
the root causes of conflict

00:36:45.090 --> 00:36:46.910
which I think some of you also mentioned.

00:36:46.910 --> 00:36:49.660
So there was a lot of alignment
there and I appreciated

00:36:50.580 --> 00:36:55.580
hearing that from a less
theoretical jargon filled way

00:36:56.160 --> 00:37:01.160
and more applicable to
people who are living

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:02.700
their lives every day.

00:37:02.700 --> 00:37:05.613
So it was great to have that framing.

00:37:06.660 --> 00:37:10.050
I said a life course approach
is what UNICEF takes.

00:37:10.050 --> 00:37:12.900
And again, I think this
is something that I heard

00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:15.510
from many of you particularly Priyanka,

00:37:15.510 --> 00:37:19.860
I noted that you mentioned solutions

00:37:19.860 --> 00:37:23.790
for age appropriate
solutions that children

00:37:23.790 --> 00:37:26.070
could take to participate.

00:37:26.070 --> 00:37:30.390
And I really like that
you brought up that point.

00:37:30.390 --> 00:37:32.430
So as Kyle Pruett mentioned,

00:37:32.430 --> 00:37:34.830
my background is in early
childhood development.

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:39.330
And we believe that peace starts early.

00:37:39.330 --> 00:37:41.490
Right from birth children are interacting

00:37:41.490 --> 00:37:44.040
with the world around them
and they are developing

00:37:44.040 --> 00:37:46.440
critical skills, emotional regulation,

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:49.770
empathy, social skills.

00:37:49.770 --> 00:37:53.430
And there are two really
key moments for building

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:55.710
these cognitive and socialization skills.

00:37:55.710 --> 00:37:57.900
And one of those is early childhood,

00:37:57.900 --> 00:38:00.990
so which would be roughly
the ages of zero to eight.

00:38:00.990 --> 00:38:03.300
And the other is in adolescence,

00:38:03.300 --> 00:38:06.570
which is I think we heard
many of our presenters today

00:38:06.570 --> 00:38:09.870
started their journeys around that time

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:11.133
which is incredible.

00:38:12.450 --> 00:38:15.720
So these periods of time
particularly in early childhood,

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:19.620
is when children are learning
attitudes and behaviors

00:38:19.620 --> 00:38:21.930
towards the other.

00:38:21.930 --> 00:38:26.130
And in resource poor contexts,

00:38:26.130 --> 00:38:29.160
the access to services that promote

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:32.130
early childhood development can also act

00:38:32.130 --> 00:38:33.450
as social equalizers.

00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:36.000
So they can help with
breaking cycles of inequities

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:40.530
and injustice, making
individuals and societies

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:42.483
more resilient to stress.

00:38:43.470 --> 00:38:46.080
Dominique, I noted your point on the use

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:48.690
of the word resilience and I
love that you brought that up.

00:38:48.690 --> 00:38:50.910
So when I say resilience now,

00:38:50.910 --> 00:38:55.530
I mean a communal societal resilience

00:38:55.530 --> 00:39:00.530
rather than an individual, let's
pretend everything is okay.

00:39:00.900 --> 00:39:04.443
And I really appreciated
that you unpacked that.

00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:09.330
So UNICEF has recently
come out with a new vision

00:39:09.330 --> 00:39:12.600
for early childhood development
which explicitly recognizes

00:39:12.600 --> 00:39:16.020
the symbiotic relationship
between peace building

00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:17.850
and fulfilling children's rights,

00:39:17.850 --> 00:39:19.983
particularly their right to development.

00:39:21.210 --> 00:39:25.230
And of course, this applies
to working with young people

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:26.340
as well.

00:39:26.340 --> 00:39:27.960
UNICEF's mandate goes up to 18,

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:30.560
but of course we recognize
the fundamental role that

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:36.800
anywhere people from adolescence
to young people, whatever,

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:40.230
that category looks different according to

00:39:40.230 --> 00:39:42.210
whichever theoretical
framework you're looking at.

00:39:42.210 --> 00:39:47.103
But essentially, we know the
activists were speaking about.

00:39:47.970 --> 00:39:52.970
And there is an underlying
trust in the fact that

00:39:59.850 --> 00:40:03.810
youth play a really catalytic
role in building peace.

00:40:03.810 --> 00:40:06.810
And therefore the work
that UNICEF tries to do

00:40:06.810 --> 00:40:10.890
is to teach peace building
and resolution skills,

00:40:10.890 --> 00:40:13.140
conflict resolution skills.

00:40:13.140 --> 00:40:15.690
To support those youth-led initiatives

00:40:15.690 --> 00:40:19.050
that we've heard about
today at a community level

00:40:19.050 --> 00:40:21.750
and at wider levels as well,

00:40:21.750 --> 00:40:25.380
and to really strengthen
the participation,

00:40:25.380 --> 00:40:28.170
the meaningful participation
of young people

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:30.960
in national dialogues and decision making

00:40:30.960 --> 00:40:32.820
and peace building processes.

00:40:32.820 --> 00:40:37.820
I think it's a fairly
common complaint really

00:40:41.700 --> 00:40:44.770
that tokenism sort of takes over

00:40:46.590 --> 00:40:49.020
rather than actual
meaningful participation.

00:40:49.020 --> 00:40:52.050
So I do believe that
that's one of the things

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:53.670
that UNICEF is trying to work on as well

00:40:53.670 --> 00:40:56.853
and I appreciate it that our
presenters spoke more to that.

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:03.360
I think what I found really motivating

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:06.810
about what all of you spoke about today

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:10.260
was the way that you
reframed these topics.

00:41:10.260 --> 00:41:14.940
I think very often topics
particularly those associated

00:41:14.940 --> 00:41:19.110
with young people tend
to be depoliticized.

00:41:19.110 --> 00:41:21.360
And I appreciated that you reframed that,

00:41:21.360 --> 00:41:23.010
you politicized it and you made that

00:41:23.010 --> 00:41:25.740
clear connection to piece.

00:41:25.740 --> 00:41:27.740
Actually I think there was a particular,

00:41:31.770 --> 00:41:36.770
yes, sorry, Penelope you
mentioned you never know

00:41:38.010 --> 00:41:40.770
which actions lead to peace.

00:41:40.770 --> 00:41:45.770
And I think that that is
something that becomes very true

00:41:48.420 --> 00:41:52.980
particularly in defining peace, right?

00:41:52.980 --> 00:41:55.983
This is again, a topic that was spoken to.

00:41:56.910 --> 00:41:59.130
So we've had things like climate change

00:41:59.130 --> 00:42:01.980
are slowly but surely becoming political,

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:05.880
but not necessarily things
like sustainable initiatives

00:42:05.880 --> 00:42:08.010
like those that you've told us about

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:09.180
Priyanka that you've started,

00:42:09.180 --> 00:42:14.110
or mental health or community,
I believe there was a phrase

00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:17.970
Dominique used the networks of care.

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:21.000
And all of these being highlighted

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:24.540
as efforts and acts of peace building

00:42:24.540 --> 00:42:27.750
is what I truly appreciated
that all of the presenters

00:42:27.750 --> 00:42:30.587
brought to the floor today.

00:42:30.587 --> 00:42:33.123
- Aditi, could I interrupt
for just a second,

00:42:34.290 --> 00:42:37.410
and as you were talking
about networks of care,

00:42:37.410 --> 00:42:39.210
I wanna remind people that their questions

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:40.740
are welcome in the chat.

00:42:40.740 --> 00:42:42.900
We will be monitoring
those and after Aditi

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:44.670
has completed her comments,

00:42:44.670 --> 00:42:47.220
we will address the
questions that you would like

00:42:47.220 --> 00:42:51.660
addressed not only to the
presenters but to Aditi as well.

00:42:51.660 --> 00:42:54.840
Speaking of networks of care,
you are all a part of it.

00:42:54.840 --> 00:42:56.340
Thank you very much, go ahead.

00:42:56.340 --> 00:42:57.596
Sorry to interrupt.

00:42:57.596 --> 00:42:58.429
- No, no worries.

00:42:58.429 --> 00:42:59.262
It was actually great timing.

00:42:59.262 --> 00:43:04.130
I was just about to move
into the discussion portion.

00:43:06.300 --> 00:43:11.300
So I noted from many of
you that you mentioned

00:43:11.970 --> 00:43:15.180
the lack of meaningful participation,

00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:18.300
or the opportunities
for that participation.

00:43:18.300 --> 00:43:22.050
And I think something that
we've seen through research

00:43:22.050 --> 00:43:26.040
is that young people
research and of course

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:28.803
hearing from experiences,

00:43:29.790 --> 00:43:33.840
young people often create
their own spaces for expression

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:38.040
and building community and
leadership and for example,

00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:41.700
using digital platforms, online advocacy.

00:43:41.700 --> 00:43:44.550
And I am just wondering
have any of you encountered

00:43:44.550 --> 00:43:47.010
the need to find your own
space in the same way?

00:43:47.010 --> 00:43:51.093
And if so, what did that
look like in your experience?

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:55.090
And over to anyone who would
like to respond to that?

00:43:59.490 --> 00:44:00.900
- I can go first maybe,

00:44:00.900 --> 00:44:03.630
I think that is what activism is about.

00:44:03.630 --> 00:44:07.200
And you mean I constantly
search for new ways,

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:10.950
new peaceful and democratic
devices I can use

00:44:10.950 --> 00:44:13.653
in order to contribute to
the changes we need to see.

00:44:14.610 --> 00:44:19.020
And a lot of these things
are spaces we make ourselves,

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:21.900
and try as a future
movement internationally

00:44:21.900 --> 00:44:24.000
and nationally in my home country

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:26.100
is a great example of this I believe.

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:28.920
And millions of young people
going out into the streets

00:44:28.920 --> 00:44:31.320
building our own platforms
and strengthening

00:44:31.320 --> 00:44:32.430
each other in our work.

00:44:32.430 --> 00:44:36.153
And so that is just one
example of like many things,

00:44:37.290 --> 00:44:39.687
many times we've build our own communities

00:44:39.687 --> 00:44:41.610
and build our own places.

00:44:41.610 --> 00:44:44.160
Yeah, I think that's the
core of being an activist.

00:44:45.510 --> 00:44:46.950
- Thank you, Penelope.

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:50.793
Did any of our other presenters
want to speak to this?

00:44:52.020 --> 00:44:54.690
- I'd just like to add
on to what Penelope said,

00:44:54.690 --> 00:44:56.190
in my personal experience,

00:44:56.190 --> 00:44:59.397
one of my first initiatives
was my Zero Waste blog.

00:44:59.397 --> 00:45:02.490
And I started blogging because it's a tool

00:45:02.490 --> 00:45:06.060
that I learned how to use
in school and I felt that

00:45:06.060 --> 00:45:08.790
social media and the digital space

00:45:08.790 --> 00:45:10.470
is where our generation is,

00:45:10.470 --> 00:45:12.030
it's where we spend most of our time.

00:45:12.030 --> 00:45:14.460
And it was how I could
engage with my peers

00:45:14.460 --> 00:45:16.830
and other young people in the Caribbean.

00:45:16.830 --> 00:45:19.350
And I started blogging about Zero Waste

00:45:19.350 --> 00:45:21.630
not only to engage with young people,

00:45:21.630 --> 00:45:23.820
but also their parents and their guardians

00:45:23.820 --> 00:45:27.030
and being able to create
something more personal

00:45:27.030 --> 00:45:29.490
and unique to our circumstances.

00:45:29.490 --> 00:45:31.950
How can we implement
these sustainable habits

00:45:31.950 --> 00:45:34.230
into our households,
our school curriculums,

00:45:34.230 --> 00:45:37.317
our communities, to make it
a Caribbean wide initiative

00:45:37.317 --> 00:45:40.800
and something that's easy
and affordable to implement.

00:45:40.800 --> 00:45:43.650
And social media and my Zero Waste blog

00:45:43.650 --> 00:45:48.480
was the perfect avenue to
achieve this because of its reach

00:45:48.480 --> 00:45:49.893
and its connectivity.

00:45:51.510 --> 00:45:53.490
- Thank you, Priyanka.

00:45:53.490 --> 00:45:55.770
And I want to appreciate
as well that you said

00:45:55.770 --> 00:46:00.630
the reach of the blog but
you also were an active part

00:46:00.630 --> 00:46:02.310
of creating that reach and I think

00:46:02.310 --> 00:46:04.893
that needs to be acknowledged.

00:46:06.510 --> 00:46:09.363
Kyle or Dominique, did you
want to come in as well?

00:46:10.828 --> 00:46:12.503
- Yeah, sure.

00:46:12.503 --> 00:46:16.320
Because I feel like especially locally,

00:46:16.320 --> 00:46:21.320
physical safe space was always
created by the community.

00:46:21.720 --> 00:46:24.300
Children and youth advocates have created

00:46:24.300 --> 00:46:26.760
their own additional
spaces inside their schools

00:46:26.760 --> 00:46:31.230
if they never felt safe
there or outside of schools.

00:46:31.230 --> 00:46:34.980
And adding on to what Priyanka
said about the digital space,

00:46:34.980 --> 00:46:38.520
I think that the digital
space has brought a component

00:46:38.520 --> 00:46:43.520
of trans nationality to youth
advocates organizing, right?

00:46:43.560 --> 00:46:46.110
You find somebody in a different country

00:46:46.110 --> 00:46:47.940
that has the same problems as you,

00:46:47.940 --> 00:46:50.760
and together you create
this transnational space

00:46:50.760 --> 00:46:52.530
of solidarity.

00:46:52.530 --> 00:46:56.400
And from there, you of course
have lovely initiatives

00:46:56.400 --> 00:46:58.830
like the one we are
participating in today, right?

00:46:58.830 --> 00:47:01.320
Because Kyle reached out to us digitally

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:04.740
and now we're all here from
different parts of the world.

00:47:04.740 --> 00:47:08.510
So yeah, I think that
constructing your own space

00:47:08.510 --> 00:47:10.920
as an activist is like Penelope said,

00:47:10.920 --> 00:47:15.603
one of the core components of advocacy.

00:47:17.160 --> 00:47:21.810
- I also like have experience
I guess making my own spaces.

00:47:21.810 --> 00:47:26.010
I started like support
groups for young people

00:47:26.010 --> 00:47:28.260
or community groups for
young people with OCD

00:47:28.260 --> 00:47:29.283
and stuff like that.

00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:34.020
But the challenge is
like remaining in silos

00:47:34.020 --> 00:47:37.440
of just young people doesn't
get the job done all the time.

00:47:37.440 --> 00:47:39.150
And it's important to have
conversations like these

00:47:39.150 --> 00:47:42.420
where we interact across
generations and with people

00:47:42.420 --> 00:47:47.027
who are in I guess, higher
up positions to make

00:47:47.940 --> 00:47:49.710
more systems level change.

00:47:49.710 --> 00:47:52.050
And also you mentioned tokenization

00:47:52.050 --> 00:47:54.120
at the very beginning of this question.

00:47:54.120 --> 00:47:59.120
And one point about
kind of like tokenizing

00:48:00.270 --> 00:48:02.130
or having youth kind of at events

00:48:02.130 --> 00:48:04.170
and maybe not knowing if you're engaging

00:48:04.170 --> 00:48:05.433
in tokenization or not,

00:48:06.390 --> 00:48:09.060
I've felt tokenized at
different events within UNICEF

00:48:09.060 --> 00:48:11.100
or the International OCD Foundation.

00:48:11.100 --> 00:48:16.100
And when that's happened,
I felt angry at times.

00:48:18.420 --> 00:48:22.350
But even though I was tokenized,

00:48:22.350 --> 00:48:23.940
it felt nice to be there

00:48:23.940 --> 00:48:26.550
and it gave me a lot of
confidence being in those spaces.

00:48:26.550 --> 00:48:29.370
And even if maybe people
didn't respect me that time

00:48:29.370 --> 00:48:31.710
or didn't like listen to
what I said that time,

00:48:31.710 --> 00:48:34.770
being there made me feel
like my voice mattered

00:48:34.770 --> 00:48:36.120
a little more than it did before.

00:48:36.120 --> 00:48:38.310
And then the next time I
was there I was able to talk

00:48:38.310 --> 00:48:40.650
a little louder and maybe
they didn't hear me that time,

00:48:40.650 --> 00:48:43.560
but then the next time I
was able to a little louder.

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:48.030
So I guess if someone is
scared that they might

00:48:48.030 --> 00:48:49.170
be engaging in tokenization,

00:48:49.170 --> 00:48:52.590
invite youth anyway
because in my experience

00:48:52.590 --> 00:48:54.450
it has only helped me
get a little bit closer

00:48:54.450 --> 00:48:56.040
to being heard.

00:48:56.040 --> 00:48:56.873
- Thank you, Kyle.

00:48:56.873 --> 00:49:00.810
And I appreciated the practical
solution there as well.

00:49:00.810 --> 00:49:03.720
That's a great way to think about it.

00:49:03.720 --> 00:49:07.920
I would love to be able to
open this up to the Q&amp;A.

00:49:07.920 --> 00:49:10.230
At the moment I don't see
anything in the Q&amp;A box,

00:49:10.230 --> 00:49:12.930
but if anyone in the
audience has a question

00:49:12.930 --> 00:49:17.790
for our presenters, please
do add it in to there.

00:49:17.790 --> 00:49:19.890
And while we're waiting
for those to come in,

00:49:19.890 --> 00:49:21.960
I'm just going to monopolize
your time some more

00:49:21.960 --> 00:49:23.760
because I find you very interesting.

00:49:24.600 --> 00:49:27.300
And I think actually many
of the things that you said

00:49:27.300 --> 00:49:29.250
lead me into this next question,

00:49:29.250 --> 00:49:33.450
this idea that creating your own spaces

00:49:33.450 --> 00:49:35.970
is fundamental to activism.

00:49:35.970 --> 00:49:40.970
And the idea that you
grow as activists I think

00:49:43.440 --> 00:49:48.440
are fairly new-ish ideas for many of us.

00:49:48.690 --> 00:49:52.590
I mean, some of you were
speaking about activism

00:49:52.590 --> 00:49:54.510
from when you were younger.

00:49:54.510 --> 00:49:58.092
I remember when I attempted
activism at the age of 10,

00:49:58.092 --> 00:50:01.650
there were no options
for digital participation

00:50:01.650 --> 00:50:04.710
or creating my own space in that same way.

00:50:04.710 --> 00:50:05.973
So I'm wondering,

00:50:09.390 --> 00:50:11.880
you've been on this
journey for a while now.

00:50:11.880 --> 00:50:16.500
You have been activists
and leaders for some time.

00:50:16.500 --> 00:50:20.640
And I'm wondering if in
the span of that time

00:50:20.640 --> 00:50:23.070
you've noticed any differences in the way

00:50:23.070 --> 00:50:25.120
that you've been able to engage

00:50:26.070 --> 00:50:28.980
or the impact that your voice has made.

00:50:28.980 --> 00:50:30.840
And I know you did a
little bit speak to this

00:50:30.840 --> 00:50:33.500
but was there anything
specific that you've noticed

00:50:33.500 --> 00:50:36.033
has changed since you started?

00:50:37.230 --> 00:50:42.230
- So, I mean, for me at
least, being an activist

00:50:42.311 --> 00:50:45.030
means reacting to the
times you're living in

00:50:45.030 --> 00:50:47.640
and changing times being present.

00:50:47.640 --> 00:50:51.480
And I constantly change my
ways and try to highlight

00:50:51.480 --> 00:50:54.720
new issues when it happens
something in our world

00:50:54.720 --> 00:50:58.440
that didn't happen a week ago that is new.

00:50:58.440 --> 00:51:01.740
Because we know that what
happens in the world around us,

00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:05.490
in society, in one way
or another happens to us.

00:51:05.490 --> 00:51:10.490
So the COVID pandemic is one
example where I needed to like

00:51:10.620 --> 00:51:13.800
change my activism in
order to reach people.

00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:18.800
We created campaigns digitally
and movements digitally

00:51:19.140 --> 00:51:21.000
instead to reach people.

00:51:21.000 --> 00:51:24.300
And at least in my part of the
world and the war in Ukraine

00:51:24.300 --> 00:51:27.480
has changed what I talk about
when I talk about activism,

00:51:27.480 --> 00:51:29.580
when I talk about climate advocacy.

00:51:29.580 --> 00:51:32.610
And we talk about the
connections between war and peace

00:51:32.610 --> 00:51:34.710
and democracy and climate change

00:51:34.710 --> 00:51:37.078
and nature changes more often now.

00:51:37.078 --> 00:51:39.090
And we talk about fossil fuel dependency,

00:51:39.090 --> 00:51:41.100
how it's important that we move away

00:51:41.100 --> 00:51:43.740
from fossil fuel dependency
in order to create peace,

00:51:43.740 --> 00:51:46.860
to not be reliant on dictatorships.

00:51:46.860 --> 00:51:49.740
So that's just examples of
things that are happening

00:51:49.740 --> 00:51:52.710
in recent years that has
kind of changed the way

00:51:52.710 --> 00:51:55.653
I do activism and what
that highlights in my work.

00:51:59.940 --> 00:52:01.560
- Thank you, Penelope.

00:52:01.560 --> 00:52:05.070
That was a great example of practically

00:52:05.070 --> 00:52:07.677
what that looks like for you.

00:52:07.677 --> 00:52:09.543
And I appreciate you sharing that.

00:52:10.500 --> 00:52:12.750
Kyle, I think I noticed
you pop up earlier.

00:52:12.750 --> 00:52:14.800
Did you have something you wanted to add?

00:52:16.020 --> 00:52:17.550
- The thing that's always difficult for me

00:52:17.550 --> 00:52:18.720
'cause I'm a bit of a luddite,

00:52:18.720 --> 00:52:22.500
is I have to like keep up on
which social media platforms

00:52:22.500 --> 00:52:25.230
and like how best to
communicate with the people

00:52:25.230 --> 00:52:26.880
I'm trying to reach.

00:52:26.880 --> 00:52:28.710
Because like a lot of the
times when I'll be working

00:52:28.710 --> 00:52:30.900
with UNICEF or the IOCDF, they'll be like,

00:52:30.900 --> 00:52:35.010
just like, let's post
something to like forbes.com

00:52:35.010 --> 00:52:36.840
and let's engage youth there.

00:52:36.840 --> 00:52:39.840
And I'll have to be like, no,
that's not what we're using.

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:41.460
Like let's start a TikTok right now

00:52:41.460 --> 00:52:44.220
and see if we can reach people that way.

00:52:44.220 --> 00:52:47.520
So that's something I always
have to keep up on and change.

00:52:47.520 --> 00:52:48.353
- Thank you.

00:52:50.220 --> 00:52:51.450
I'm not sure if our other speakers

00:52:51.450 --> 00:52:53.733
had anything they wanted to add.

00:52:55.800 --> 00:52:57.660
- Yeah, I would just
like to add something.

00:52:57.660 --> 00:52:59.463
I think for me personally,

00:53:04.550 --> 00:53:05.930
I've learned more about my audience

00:53:05.930 --> 00:53:07.860
and the changes that they experience.

00:53:07.860 --> 00:53:10.410
I think especially in the Caribbean,

00:53:10.410 --> 00:53:13.830
the leadership has always
been traditionally top down

00:53:13.830 --> 00:53:17.430
with youth participation may not be always

00:53:17.430 --> 00:53:18.900
meaningful or long term.

00:53:18.900 --> 00:53:21.120
And I think along with
that I've realized that

00:53:21.120 --> 00:53:22.710
as activists and advocates,

00:53:22.710 --> 00:53:27.030
we can only reach so much due
to this huge knowledge gap.

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:30.660
And this made me really
realize the importance

00:53:30.660 --> 00:53:33.210
for education and encouragement,

00:53:33.210 --> 00:53:36.780
understanding how we are uniquely affected

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:39.990
and how we are contributors yet victims.

00:53:39.990 --> 00:53:42.390
And I think really
understanding that has played

00:53:42.390 --> 00:53:44.910
a major role in how
I've tweaked my activism

00:53:44.910 --> 00:53:48.000
as Penelope was saying,
adjusted my own advocacy

00:53:48.000 --> 00:53:51.600
to reach my audience and young people here

00:53:51.600 --> 00:53:54.750
understanding how we're
affected by natural disasters,

00:53:54.750 --> 00:53:59.733
flooding, and what we can do
in order to mitigate and adapt.

00:54:01.980 --> 00:54:02.813
- Thank you, Priyanka.

00:54:02.813 --> 00:54:06.750
And I think your answers have
also helped conceptualize

00:54:06.750 --> 00:54:11.750
what exactly activism can
look like across the board

00:54:11.760 --> 00:54:12.690
which is great.

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:14.280
I am conscious of the time,

00:54:14.280 --> 00:54:17.430
so I think I'd like to take
one question from the audience

00:54:17.430 --> 00:54:21.000
before passing over to
Kyle Pruett to close.

00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:24.510
So we have a question from
Paul Stevenson which is,

00:54:24.510 --> 00:54:27.360
what guidance or experiences
do the panel have

00:54:27.360 --> 00:54:30.360
for strategies that help
to sustain their movements

00:54:30.360 --> 00:54:31.800
or spaces?

00:54:31.800 --> 00:54:34.680
And Paul clarifies that this
could include sustaining

00:54:34.680 --> 00:54:39.543
momentum and motivation as
much as funding and resourcing.

00:54:41.010 --> 00:54:42.093
- I can take over.

00:54:43.230 --> 00:54:47.005
So you said it really well Paul there,

00:54:47.005 --> 00:54:49.443
funding is really vital for our movements.

00:54:50.280 --> 00:54:55.280
And moreover, funding that
doesn't alter the movement

00:54:55.620 --> 00:54:58.740
because sometimes we're
conditioned to the ideas

00:54:58.740 --> 00:55:02.760
that we can present in our
movements to the funding.

00:55:02.760 --> 00:55:05.850
And again, what I would say
again in the momentum part

00:55:05.850 --> 00:55:07.950
would be children and youth advocates

00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:10.800
need to understand that they're
making a difference, right?

00:55:10.800 --> 00:55:13.920
So trying to engage them
in very meaningful ways

00:55:13.920 --> 00:55:16.590
and seeing how they make that impact

00:55:16.590 --> 00:55:18.360
is going to keep momentum up.

00:55:18.360 --> 00:55:20.820
Also mentorship, I know
that it's very important

00:55:20.820 --> 00:55:23.550
and I talked about it
in my speech as well,

00:55:23.550 --> 00:55:27.830
to have somebody who has been
through different experiences

00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:33.300
and can mentor the movement
without diminishing

00:55:33.300 --> 00:55:38.300
the light of each person
who is part of the movement.

00:55:39.390 --> 00:55:42.270
And I would also say that
it's important to be mindful

00:55:42.270 --> 00:55:44.820
of the mental health of
children and of youth advocates.

00:55:44.820 --> 00:55:48.570
I know that I was ready to
do absolutely everything

00:55:48.570 --> 00:55:51.690
for the movement and that
meant a lot of burnout

00:55:51.690 --> 00:55:54.810
and not having the tools
to navigate spaces.

00:55:54.810 --> 00:55:58.500
And so I would see a lot
of children being pushed

00:55:58.500 --> 00:56:02.280
to their limits by activism and by adults

00:56:02.280 --> 00:56:05.190
who didn't really know how
to approach this, right?

00:56:05.190 --> 00:56:07.590
So make sure that when
you're engaging children

00:56:07.590 --> 00:56:09.940
and to keep this momentum up,

00:56:09.940 --> 00:56:12.690
you give them the space to also rest,

00:56:12.690 --> 00:56:15.420
to also play, to also enjoy childhood.

00:56:15.420 --> 00:56:17.163
So that's from my part.

00:56:18.900 --> 00:56:20.050
- Thank you, Dominique.

00:56:21.480 --> 00:56:24.130
Would any of the other
panelists like to add to that?

00:56:30.210 --> 00:56:31.923
No, fantastic.

00:56:33.390 --> 00:56:36.420
In which case Kyle Pruett,
I will hand it over to you.

00:56:36.420 --> 00:56:37.870
Thank you very much everyone.

00:56:39.450 --> 00:56:40.782
- Thank you, Aditi.

00:56:40.782 --> 00:56:44.130
I am sorry to say this
wonderful conversation

00:56:44.130 --> 00:56:48.210
which in my view is just
beginning has to come to close

00:56:48.210 --> 00:56:50.070
because we've run out of time.

00:56:50.070 --> 00:56:53.700
A few final thoughts, one
about the concept of resilience

00:56:53.700 --> 00:56:56.250
which Dominique and
others have talked about.

00:56:56.250 --> 00:56:58.260
In my own view as a child psychiatrist,

00:56:58.260 --> 00:57:01.500
I have seen resilience massively misused.

00:57:01.500 --> 00:57:03.810
It is not an individual trait of a child.

00:57:03.810 --> 00:57:06.330
In fact, that can be so politicized

00:57:06.330 --> 00:57:07.770
that if it is an individual trait

00:57:07.770 --> 00:57:09.330
then why should governments do anything

00:57:09.330 --> 00:57:11.310
to help children who are vulnerable?

00:57:11.310 --> 00:57:16.310
Resilience is a property
of an environment,

00:57:16.530 --> 00:57:18.810
not just simply the child that's in it,

00:57:18.810 --> 00:57:20.670
but the state around it,

00:57:20.670 --> 00:57:23.070
the people that love that
child and protect it as well.

00:57:23.070 --> 00:57:25.560
So let's not be simple-minded when we come

00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:26.790
to talk about resilience.

00:57:26.790 --> 00:57:29.550
And our panel obviously is way ahead

00:57:29.550 --> 00:57:31.740
of many of us in understanding that.

00:57:31.740 --> 00:57:35.430
I would like to conclude our
comments with an invitation

00:57:35.430 --> 00:57:40.430
to think together about
how ECPC could use such a

00:57:41.760 --> 00:57:44.460
youth working group to move forward.

00:57:44.460 --> 00:57:48.120
Their passion, their
competence, and their ability

00:57:48.120 --> 00:57:52.290
to really provoke us to
talk about the right things

00:57:52.290 --> 00:57:53.580
makes perfect sense.

00:57:53.580 --> 00:57:55.953
Follow us on the ECPC newsletter.

00:57:58.500 --> 00:58:00.690
The third lecture in
this series will feature

00:58:00.690 --> 00:58:03.840
Sherrie Westin who is the
President of Sesame Workshop

00:58:03.840 --> 00:58:07.650
and Dr. Hiro Yoshikawa
who will be talking about

00:58:07.650 --> 00:58:09.540
a variety of interventions that have been

00:58:09.540 --> 00:58:13.240
phenomenally successful in migratory

00:58:16.560 --> 00:58:18.240
and refugee camps around the world.

00:58:18.240 --> 00:58:19.740
Thank you for being with us.

00:58:19.740 --> 00:58:21.030
Thank you to the panel.

00:58:21.030 --> 00:58:23.430
What a contribution they
have made to us today.

00:58:23.430 --> 00:58:24.263
Thank you.

00:58:25.554 --> 00:58:28.137
(upbeat music)

